# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which converts text files to
# CRLF on checkout. Two things in this repo break when that happens, and both
# break in ways that do not point at line endings:
#
#   internal/config/testdata/golden_config.json is compared byte for byte
#   against what the parser produces, which is always LF. On a fresh Windows
#   clone that test fails with a diff showing two identical-looking documents.
#
#   scripts/*.sh are executed on Linux hosts. A CRLF shebang line makes the
#   kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return, and
#   the error it produces names the interpreter, not the line ending.
#
# Declaring the endings here settles it for every clone regardless of local
# git configuration. Nothing changes in the repository itself: every file is
# already stored with LF, so this only affects what lands in the working tree.

* text=auto

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.go    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf

# Windows-native, and the only file here a Windows tool opens directly.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
